<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Gentile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Gentile was an Italian educator, philosopher and politician, born in Castelvetrano, and one of the primary founders of fascism. With Benedetto Croce he was a leading exponent of Italian idealism, and he devised his own system of actual idealism. Called the philosopher of fascism by himself and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, he wrote the 1925 Manifesto of the Fascist Intellectuals and part of The Doctrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> of 1932. As Minister for Public Education he introduced the Gentile Reform of 1923, and he was the first editor of the Italian Encyclopedia. He followed Mussolini into the Italian Social Republic and was assassinated by partisans of the Italian resistance in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-gentile.jpg" alt="Giovanni Gentile" width="330" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Gentile_Portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Italian opponents of Mussolini coined the word “totalitarian” in the 1920s, and Mussolini and the philosopher <b>Giovanni Gentile</b> adopted it with pride.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In The Doctrine of Fascism (1932), partly ghostwritten by <b>Giovanni Gentile</b>, Mussolini wrote that the fascist state was all-embracing: “outside of it no human or spiritual values can exist”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
